runsc: keep /tmp on the root filesystem when it has no overlay

What: runsc no longer mounts its internal tmpfs on an empty or missing /tmp
when the container's root filesystem is writable, served by the gofer and has
no overlay (its gofer mount config has no upper layer, as with
--overlay2=none). /tmp is then a directory of the root filesystem, and what
the container writes there goes to its filesystem on the host, as under runc.
With an overlay on the root (runsc's default root:self, or root:memory) and
with a read-only root, runsc mounts the tmpfs as before; an explicit /tmp
mount and a non-empty /tmp are left alone as before.

Why: Orbit runs every sandbox with --overlay2=none so that a container's writes
stay in its Docker filesystem, as under runc: docker commit keeps them in a
snapshot, docker cp reads them, and they take disk, not the container's
memory. The internal tmpfs broke that for the /tmp of an image whose /tmp is
empty, such as python:3.12-slim: 300 MB written there was charged to the
container (docker stats 307 MiB, Shmem 293 MB), and docker commit dropped
it. This follows the overlay setting rather than a new flag: an operator who
turns the root's overlay off asks for the container's writes to land in its
filesystem on the host, and writes to /tmp are among them. With the overlay
on, the container's writes stay in the sandbox anyway, and a read-only root
keeps the writable tmpfs it had, since nothing it writes reaches the host.

Verified:
- gVisor's unit tests in its builder container: the new TestMountTmp
  (runsc/boot/loader_test.go) mounts a container's filesystems (mountAll)
  over a root directory with an empty /tmp and checks the filesystem at
  /tmp: tmpfs with a memory overlay on the root, the root's own gofer
  filesystem (9p) without one, and tmpfs for a read-only root without one.
  Without this patch the case without an overlay fails ("/tmp is on tmpfs,
  want 9p"); with it, all three and the rest of //runsc/boot:boot_test pass.
- runsc run as the host's root (kernel 7.0, --network=none,
  --ignore-cgroups) on an OCI bundle whose root directory has an empty /tmp,
  owned by root with mode 1777: with --overlay2=none, before this patch
  /proc/mounts listed "none /tmp tmpfs rw,mode=01777", statfs of /tmp
  returned TMPFS_MAGIC (0x1021994), and a file written to /tmp did not reach
  the bundle's root directory on the host while one written to / did; with
  it, /tmp was no mount, statfs returned the root's V9FS_MAGIC (0x1021997),
  and both files were in the root directory on the host. With the default
  overlay, before and after, /tmp was the tmpfs and neither file reached the
  host; with --overlay2=none and a read-only root, /tmp was the tmpfs.

